The JavaScript/TypeScript binding is one source that runs on Deno, Bun, and
experimental Node: all runtime coupling lives behind a thin FFI/socket seam that
loads the matching adapter at startup. It loads the installed shared pancake
archives over the host's FFI and mirrors the à-la-carte archive set as a Deno
workspace of two packages. The core
[`bindings/ts/stack/mod.ts`](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/ts/stack/mod.ts)
(`@pancake/stack`, over `libpancake_ocapn`) presents a `Peer` class, with the
built-in transports `@pancake/stack/transport/tcp` and
`@pancake/stack/transport/inproc`; the optional encrypted-QUIC transport is the
separate `@pancake/iroh` package, reached lazily by the core's `connect` for an
`iroh` locator. Each opens only its own archive. Wire values arrive as
discriminated unions, so a delivery is a value you switch on with the type
narrowing you already expect from TypeScript.

## The same round-trip

The round-trip the [Start building]($link.page('start')) page walks through in Zig —
connect, fetch a capability by name, call it, and handle the reply — reads like this in
TypeScript. Starting `serve` runs the inbound loop so the reply arrives.

```ts
import { connect } from "@pancake/stack";

const conn = await connect("ocapn://127.0.0.1:9099.tcp"); // the partner's locator
conn.serve().catch(() => {});
const greeter = await conn.fetch("greeter"); // a capability, by name
try {
  const reply = await conn.send(greeter, "greet", ["world"]);
  console.log(reply);
} catch (reason) {
  console.error("the greeter refused:", reason);
}
conn.close();
```

## Async

The async surface returns native `Promise`s and supports `using`, so a connection
cleans itself up at the end of a block. An `AsyncConnection` runs the peer over
the host runtime's own sockets. You read events as a stream with
`for await (const event of conn)` and await a result directly with
`await vat.send(ref, verb, args)`: no polling, no callback registration.

## Actors

The actor layer is a `Vat` over `(verb, args) => Argument` callbacks. A local actor
is a plain function; an eventual-send to a partner-hosted reference resolves the
returned promise when the answer settles.

## Dogfood and example

```sh
zig build deno-dogfood    # the Deno consumer against the pancake archives
zig build bun-dogfood     # the same source, on Bun
```

The full surface lives in the
[TypeScript binding README](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/ts/README.md),
and the
[rooms chat client](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/examples/apps/rooms/deno/room_client.ts)
builds a small capability-secure room on this surface.
